=== Philanthropy <span class="anchor" id="Philanthrophy"></span>===
Many stories are told of Abd al-Rahman's personal generosity. He once furnished Muhammad's army with 1,500 camels. He bequeathed 400 ''dinars'' to the survivors of Badr and a large legacy to the widows of Muhammad.{{citation needed|date=January 2015}}

[[Al-Dhahabi|Dhahabi]] reported that Abd al-Rahman brought a caravan of about 700 merchant-camels into Medina.{{sfn|Zaman|1970||p=40}} [[Aisha bint Abi Bakr|Aisha]] remarked, "I have heard Allah's Messenger say: 'I have seen Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf entering Paradise crawling.'" This was repeated to Abd al-Rahman, who replied: "If I could, I would certainly like to enter Paradise standing. I swear to you, that this entire caravan with all its merchandise, I will give in charity." And so he did.<ref>[http://musliminc.com/abdel-rahman-ibn-awf-the-richest-muslim-who-bought-his-way-to-jannah-4012 Abdel Rahman Ibn Awf, The Richest Muslim Who Bought His Way to Jannah] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151211205821/http://musliminc.com/abdel-rahman-ibn-awf-the-richest-muslim-who-bought-his-way-to-jannah-4012 |date=2015-12-11 }}</ref>

Abdel Rahman also reportedly gave in charity 50,000 [[gold dinar]]s of his personal wealth.<ref name="Elite" />

=== Sunni view ===
[[Sunni]]s regard him as one of {{transliteration|ar|al-ʿashara al-mubashsharūn}}—the [[The ten to whom Paradise was promised|ten companions]] that Muhammad prophesied would enter Paradise.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://sunnah.com/urn/736260 |title=Jami' At-Tirmidhi |access-date=2021-03-11 |archive-date=2021-05-06 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210506063543/https://sunnah.com/urn/736260 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

